>
    <p class="lede">Canada is the future of the planet.</p>
    <p>
        He called the afternoon a kickoff, not the multi-day launch a claim of
        this size would usually require. Canada2080 is a long horizon. The
        point of the day was to gather people who believe in similar
        possibilities, start the conversations, and hold many more.
    </p>
    <p>
        Looking forward from today, he named what is already in hand: land,
        freshwater, rare earths and biomaterials, clean power, research
        institutions, and a strong zero-to-one. The failure is
        commercialization and scale. Intellectual property goes to foreign
        corporations. Educated people leave. Raw materials are sold instead of
        becoming local supply chains. He called that a trade of intellectual
        culture and resource value for cheap money. We specialize, he said, as
        a plantation for the world.
    </p>
    <p>
        His picture of how the value leaves: American industrialists built the
        oil pipelines so the industry stayed in America. Today, entrepreneurs
        make the money in the Bay Area, then sometimes fund training
        institutions in Canada whose talent is funnelled elsewhere.
    </p>
    <p>
        The ambition in front of that failure is resilient living systems,
        manufacturing brought back, an integrated supply chain, and chips made
        here. By then, he said, they would probably be quantum chips. It took
        Taiwan 40 years, in his account, to lead in chip manufacturing. These
        things are feasible if the country aims at them.
    </p>
    <p>
        Then he looked backward from 2080. On current models, 3.8 billion
        people would be living outside the human climate niche and would need
        to go somewhere. The equal-earth projection, recently adopted by the
        UN, shows little land in the Southern Hemisphere. Climate-based
        migration moves north, toward countries less affected by rising
        coastlines. He said Canada will host the world's people.
        The population that arrives, he said, is what makes the density
        patterns he associates with Asia possible here, and what supplies the
        workforce for a domestic supply chain.
    </p>
    <p>
        Selling resources and culture brings revenue and exchange. Retaining
        capability keeps the means to build and operate. Retaining ownership
        and decision rights keeps the choice of direction. Retaining learning
        and reinvestment keeps one success connected to the next. He said
        Canada needs all four.
    </p>
    <p>
        AI and infrastructure are tightly coupled, on different timescales.
        Since industrialization, scale has required energy. With agentic AI, he
        argued, scale also requires compute, and still more energy. He does not
        know the next turn. It could be quantum computing, or reconfigurable
        surfaces in advanced materials. He quoted Carney, from forward guidance
        the month before: "Controlling our energy means controlling our
        destiny."
    </p>
    <p>
        The form he imagined is neighbourhoods that generate their own energy
        and compute, produce their own food, and manage their water. A
        household keeps health data, finances, and private records on local
        compute, while that same capacity serves the connected system for less
        sensitive work. He ended the opening on dial-up and cable modems, as a
        computer science graduate from Waterloo. Every modem was a physical
        piece of a network with no form. Parents who had bought gaming
        computers, he said, had already invested in Canada's infrastructure and
        sovereign assets. The work has started, in small steps.
    </p>
    <p>
        He closed in English and in Chinese. An aunt had told him these events
        are always in English, and difficult for her. His parents were in the
        room. He asked guests to carry the ideas into their own communities. A
        He closed the session with two sayings that capture the essence of
        the day. A plaque at Waterloo's Conrad School of Business says that
        to go fast you go alone, and to go far you go together. The value, he
        said, has to come back to the people who struggled toward it. It takes
        grit and luck. He used "Better run than curse the road," and
        与其感慨路难行，不如即刻出发. We, the North, are a strong people. We
        know tough winters, how to keep stock, and how to provide for one
        another. Winter will pass, and we will welcome spring.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <div class="transcript-columns">
            <div>
                <h3>Opening</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                Good afternoon. To everyone joining in person and online, thank
                you for coming to the kickoff of Canada2080.
            </p>
            <p>
                Normally, a launch at this scale would be a multi-day event.
                Each of the guests invited today would have a much longer
                keynote, followed by hour-long panels, if we were to do this
                properly.
            </p>
            <p>
                As the name suggests, Canada2080 is a long-horizon initiative.
                My intention today, in calling a kickoff, is to gather people
                who believe in similar possibilities, to start conversations,
                and to hold many more events.
            </p>
            <p>
                I have been thinking about bold ambitions, and claims, to rally
                behind and to put purpose to the struggle. For Canada to be one
                of the most prosperous nations by 2080, and to say that Canada
                is the future of planet Earth.
            </p>
            <p>Let us begin by looking forward from today.</p>
            <p>
                Canada has strong foundations. Land, freshwater, rare earths
                and biomaterials, clean power, and a home for some of the most
                innovative education and research institutions in the world. We
                have the natural capital, and a strong zero-to-one in almost
                everything.
            </p>
            <p>
                We also have our issues. Today, Canadian firms often struggle
                to commercialize and scale. Intellectual property goes to
                foreign corporations. Educated people leave Canada for
                opportunities elsewhere. We sell our raw materials instead of
                building the capabilities and the supply chains for a thriving
                local economy.
            </p>
            <p>
                The American industrialists were smart. Back then, they built
                the oil pipelines so the industries stayed in America. Today,
                the smartest entrepreneurs and innovators flock to the Bay Area,
                make the money, and then sometimes feel it is time to
                contribute. They bring money back to build more training
                institutions, which end up building talent in Canada to funnel
                elsewhere in the world.
            </p>
            <p>
                We are trading our intellectual culture and our resources, the
                value, for cheap money. We specialize as a plantation for the
                world, without deliberately building the infrastructure for
                ourselves.
            </p>
            <p>
                But we are rich in this zero-to-one, and the opportunities lie
                in front of us. To build resilient living systems. To bring
                back manufacturing and an integrated supply chain. We can make
                our own compute chips, although by then they will probably be
                quantum chips. It took Taiwan 40 years to become a leader in
                chip manufacturing. These big, ambitious things are feasible if
                we aim for them.
            </p>
            <p>Now, let us look backwards from the future.</p>
            <p>Canada is the future of this planet.</p>
            <p>
                By 2080, 3.8 billion people will be living outside the human
                climate niche, based on current models. These people need to go
                somewhere. If you look at the world map, the equal-earth
                projection recently adopted by the UN, there is not a lot of
                land in the Southern Hemisphere. Climate-based migration will
                move northward, to countries that are not as affected by rising
                coastlines. That leaves Canada and Russia to host the world's
                people.
            </p>
            <p>
                What is interesting about this future is that, some time along
                the way, there will be climate-based immigration and a
                corresponding increase in population in Canada. That enables
                business models and patterns in Asia which depend on a higher
                population density that cannot be replicated here. We will also
                have the workforce to support that integrated supply chain.
            </p>
            <p>So there is a picture. What can we do to actualize it?</p>
            <p>
                A country can sell resources and still lose the higher-value
                capability around them. It can export culture and still lose
                the ownership, distribution, and learning that allow culture to
                compound. It can produce brilliant research and still see the
                decisions, production, and reinvestment happen elsewhere.
            </p>
            <p>The distinction is simple, even when the work is hard.</p>
            <p>
                Selling resources and culture brings revenue and exchange.
                Retaining capability keeps the means to build and operate.
                Retaining ownership and decision rights keeps the right to
                choose a direction. Retaining learning and reinvestment keeps
                each success connected to the next generation of capacity.
            </p>
            <p>Canada needs all four.</p>
            <p>
                Our first panel is on AI, and the second is on infrastructure.
                I believe these two are tightly coupled, although we address
                them on different timescales.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since industrialization, everything we do at scale requires
                energy. AI is critical at this moment because, with agentic AI,
                I will argue that everything that happens at scale from this
                point on will require compute, and with that an even greater
                demand for energy. I always tell people I do not know what the
                next turn will be. It could be quantum computing, or
                reconfigurable surfaces in some advanced materials. Whatever it
                is, it will need even more energy, even more compute, and
                whatever the new thing will be. That is building strong
                foundations.
            </p>
            <p>
                This is in line with our current national strategy. Carney
                stated, in his forward guidance this past month, and I quote:
                "Controlling our energy means controlling our destiny."
            </p>
            <p>
                We need to build capacity in Canada in a way that is resilient.
                Resilient systems are made of resilient parts, with connections
                across scales. AI is making complex systems possible at a small
                scale, because management is cheaper. So perhaps we could build
                new neighbourhoods that generate their own energy and compute,
                produce their own food, and manage their water. These resilient
                parts come together as a resilient society.
            </p>
            <p>
                I will end this introduction with some of my current work, from
                decentralized energy, and bringing that to decentralized
                compute. I imagine a prosperous future in which every household
                can manage some of that complexity. Your family's health data,
                financial information, and digitized private assets sit on local
                compute in the household, while that same capacity serves your
                connected systems for less sensitive work.
            </p>
            <p>
                Show of hands: how many people remember dial-up modems? That
                nostalgic connection noise, which I will not replicate, though I
                could. It is noisy and it takes forever. Then came cable
                modems. Sometimes you had to unplug them, plug them back in,
                and wait before you were online. Why is it so slow?
            </p>
            <p>
                As a computer science graduate from Waterloo, I will tell you
                what is fascinating about these things. Every piece of those
                modems was a physical part of the internet. A concrete presence
                of something with no form. It has to communicate and join a
                complex network of billions of devices. So I understand why it
                takes some time. And I still complain that it is so slow,
                because users do not care about that.
            </p>
            <p>
                Why I bring it up: I see younger people here, and parents with
                children. If you bought, for yourself or for your children, one
                of these now very expensive gaming machines, I thank you for
                investing in Canada's infrastructure and sovereign assets. We
                have already started, in small steps. Let us turn to AI, and to
                our guests. Thank you.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 id="closing">Closing</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                Thank you, Kritika. Thank you to all of our guest speakers for
                a barrage of information. Time flew by.
            </p>
            <p>
                We are in Markham. One of my aunts has always told me that
                these events are always in English, and that it is difficult
                for her. My parents are here too, and I know it is difficult
                for them to understand what I have been saying.
            </p>
            <p>
                So I will try to close, and to make the invitation, in both
                languages, so people can know how important this is, and how
                noble the pursuit is. If you are willing to invest the time,
                help do that in your own communities.
            </p>
            <p>
                我燕姨一直说这些活动都是用英文讲的，她其实也听不太懂。所以今天我就试试中英双开。我小学三年级的语文水平啊，大家见笑了。
            </p>
            <p>
                In the Conrad School of Business at Waterloo, there is a framed
                plaque that says, "If you want to go fast, go alone. If you
                want to go far, go together." I believe that sums up today. An
                initiative of this magnitude requires coalitions, partnerships,
                and a common belief in a future to co-create and actualize, and
                to benefit from the value realized by that future, for all
                those who struggled toward it.
            </p>
            <p>
                我们滑铁卢大学的一个商学院里面有这么一个poster，说独行快，众行远。我个人觉得这句话很好，也很符合今天我们讲的那些东西。要想把一个50年的远景做出来，不是一小群人可以办到的事情。我们需要团体和合作，以及共识的理想，去实现这些未来。才能在这个未来成真之后，能把它带来的财富给予在实践路上的这些人。
            </p>
            <p>
                Actualizing the future, and building Canada into a strong,
                sovereign nation on the global stage. For all our excitement
                and passion, it is a long and arduous journey. It is difficult.
                It takes grit and luck. As the old saying goes, fortune favours
                the bold.
            </p>
            <p>
                There is another phrase I like. I believe it is a Western
                idiom: "Better run than curse the road." Honestly, it sounds a
                lot cooler in Chinese.
            </p>
            <p>
                Providence favours those who persist despite failures, continue
                despite adversity, and still carry on despite being tired. They
                are favoured. We are favoured, because we have a kind of
                resilience that comes from having a deeper purpose, a why that
                propels us to press onward and stay restless in the pursuit.
            </p>
            <p>
                So difficult as it is, the difficulty itself is of no concern.
                We knew about the difficulties before we started.
            </p>
            <p>
                我们这次打出这么大的口号，要把加拿大这个国家做成世界强国。难。真的很难。我们需要很大的韧性。而且光是韧性还远远不够，我们还需要运气。但话说回来了，有些运气是可以人为的。上天眷恋什么样的人呢？上天眷恋那些失败后还能继续的人，那些受挫折但仍然坚持的人，和那些累、但脚却停不下来的人。这些人会被眷恋，因为这些人他们停不下来。他们停不下来，是因为他们背后有更大的意志和理想驱动着他们。
            </p>
            <p>
                所以难不是问题。因为我们在启程的时候就已经知道这条路很难了。
            </p>
            <p>"Better run than curse the road."</p>
            <p>
                与其感慨路难行，不如即刻出发。让我们风雨兼程，笃行不怠。也祝我们一往无前，所向披靡。
            </p>
            <p>"Better run than curse the road."</p>
            <p>
                We, the North, are a strong people. We know about tough
                winters, how to be resourceful, how to keep stock, how to
                provide for one another in tough times, for when the table
                turns and we need help. It takes a community. But winter will
                pass, and we will welcome spring.
            </p>
            <p>
                I invite all of you to join this initiative, and together we
                will build a sustainable, abundant, and resilient Canada. Thank
                you.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
